<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumarajiva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">344–413</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumarajiva was a Buddhist monk and translator from the oasis kingdom of Kucha, the son of an Indian father and a Kuchean princess. Captured by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-guang">Lü Guang</a> in 384, he spent years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a> of Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 401. There, with hundreds of assistants, he translated many Buddhist texts into Chinese, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> and the Diamond Sutra, in versions that remain standard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumarajiva.jpg" alt="Kumarajiva" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yoshi Canopus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumarajiva_at_Kizil_Caves,_Kuqa_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
